    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Atmospheric chemistry",
    "problem": "Scientific disagreement exists over whether declining fossil fuel emissions or changes in microbial sources are responsible for the observed slowdown in the growth rate of atmospheric methane, creating uncertainty in understanding its global budget.",
    "method": "The authors analyze a 26-year global record of atmospheric ethane concentrations to quantify its declining emission rate, and use this trend with methane-to-ethane emission ratios to infer that reduced fugitive fossil fuel emissions are a major contributor to the slowing growth rate of atmospheric methane.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze a 26-year record of global atmospheric ethane to quantify its long-term decline and use this trend to infer the concurrent reduction in fugitive methane emissions from fossil fuels, thereby explaining the slowdown in methane's atmospheric growth rate.",
